        ///
        ///
        /// CAMPAIGN_COMPLETED — the campaign is complete (the UTC date is after its EndOn), so it and its assets are read-only. Refuses UpdateCampaign, BatchCreateCampaignAsset, UpdateCampaignAsset, DeleteCampaignAsset and StartCampaignAssetActivationTask; never DeactivateCampaignAsset. Not retryable, and not fixable — EndOn cannot be moved once it has passed, so nothing the caller does will make the write succeed. ResourceId carries the campaign identifier. ⚠️ This is the one conflict reason whose condition never clears, which sits awkwardly with ConflictException promising a state that may change. It is a conflict rather than a validation failure because the request is well formed and would have succeeded a moment earlier — the resource's state is what refuses it, which is what this exception is for. Contrast ASSET_NOT_STARTABLE, which moved to ValidationException precisely because it is a property of the asset's type rather than of its state. ⚠️ COMPLETED also exists as a CampaignAssetExportTaskStatus value, on a different axis. That one means an export archive finished assembling. The two are unrelated and must not be unified. CAMPAIGN_HAS_ACTIVE_ASSETS — from DeleteCampaign: at least one of the campaign's assets is live, and deleting the campaign would orphan what the delivery platform still serves or sends. Deactivate those assets with DeactivateCampaignAsset, then retry. The message names them. ResourceId carries the campaign identifier. CAMPAIGN_ACTIVATION_IN_PROGRESS — from StartCampaignAssetActivationTask and DeleteCampaign, refusing the whole request: one activation task runs on a campaign at a time. ResourceId carries the holding task's id, so a caller can poll GetCampaignAssetActivationTask rather than guess. Retryable once that task reaches a terminal status. SERVICE_LINKED_ROLE_DELETION_IN_PROGRESS — from StartCampaignAssetActivationTask, refusing the whole request: this service's service-linked role is being deleted, so no asset may go live. Not a lease conflict: ResourceId is unset and there is no task to poll. Retryable after a short wait; if the role was deleted, the next activation recreates it. LAYOUT_LOCKED_AFTER_ACTIVATION — from UpdateCampaignAsset, refusing a Layout change on a multi-layout asset that has activated successfully at least once (DG-044). The published object is that layout's, so switching would leave the asset describing a layout that is not the one live. Not fixable: the asset keeps its layout for life. A failed activation does not lock it. Re-sending the layout already selected is not a change and is accepted. ResourceId carries the asset identifier. ASSET_START_IN_PROGRESS — also from UpdateCampaignAsset, refusing any edit to an asset the campaign's running activation task has accepted and not yet finished: the run publishes the content it read, so an edit accepted mid-run would either be published unvalidated or never published. Nothing is written. Retryable once the asset's outcome on the task (GetCampaignAssetActivationTask) is terminal; the asset's siblings may still be running. ResourceId carries the asset identifier. ASSET_ALREADY_ACTIVE — from UpdateCampaignAsset, refusing any change to a live email or social post, content or schedule. Deactivate it first; the next activation sends everything current. Landing pages and documents are edited live and converge by re-activation. ResourceId carries the asset identifier. ASSET_NOT_DEACTIVATABLE — from DeactivateCampaignAsset, for an asset that can't be stopped: a document, banner or landing page until the campaign has completed, when it becomes removable. Until then, update it and activate it again. ResourceId carries the asset identifier. ASSET_HAS_LOCKED_SENDS — from DeactivateCampaignAsset, within an hour of the asset's send (a social post's publish), when it can no longer be withdrawn. ASSET_ALREADY_DELIVERED — the same, once that time has passed: a sent email or a published post cannot be recalled. Neither is retryable. Three va